{"id": "dig-pa-tickets-1", "state": "PA", "tier": "shelf", "title": "Pennsylvania 811: notice 3 to 10 business days, no-response rule, complex projects", "topic": "811 tickets: types, ticket life, renewals and updates", "text": "Pennsylvania notice (73 P.S. 180(2.1)): \"not less than three nor more than ten business days in advance of beginning excavation or demolition work.\" A business day is any day but Saturday, Sunday or a legal holiday, midnight to midnight. The lawful start date is the scheduled start date on the ticket. The statute sets no ticket life in days; it is not confirmed here. It does require a new notice if you vacate the worksite for more than two business days without other arrangements with the facility owners (clause 14). No response: if a facility owner \"fails to respond to the excavator's timely request,\" the excavator may proceed, not before the lawful start date and using due care (clause 5). PA One Call's response system (KARL) shows each owner's code, such as \"001 Clear\" and \"003 Field Marked\"; \"999 Did not respond\" is a violation by the owner. Find an unmarked or wrongly marked line when you arrive: renotify PA One Call; if the owner gives nothing useful within three hours you may proceed with care (clause 20). Complex projects (more than one locate request, or long schedules): a complex project ticket needs 10 business days' notice and a preconstruction meeting; they are entered through Coordinate PA, as are design notifications.", "source": "73 P.S. 176, 180, 182.10 (Underground Utility Line Protection Law; FindLaw copy, current as of Jan 1, 2026); PA One Call presentation to the PUC (Sept 2025)", "url": "https://codes.findlaw.com/pa/title-73-ps-trade-and-commerce/pa-st-sect-73-180/", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "Current as of January 1, 2026", "confidence": "medium", "kind": "summary", "license": "gov-state", "unconfirmed": "ticket life in days"}]$kb$::jsonb) e
